    {
      "id": "Ugy6CuP9hb55RH7cBtV4AaABAg",
      "author": "@Brett.McMillin",
      "authorImage": "https://yt3.ggpht.com/ytc/AIdro_lt4Qaz4Xm8HbIjxDMr8ToC0xGnZCzkVLctx9-_pB7l1Us=s48-c-k-c0x00ffffff-no-rj",
      "text": "But why is the G pronounced like a K? Couldn’t they just spell it with a K? Considering they don’t use the Latin alphabet",
      "likeCount": 0,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-03T01:29:37Z",
      "replyCount": 2,
      "replies": [
        {
          "id": "Ugy6CuP9hb55RH7cBtV4AaABAg.AcjCweTqbBMAcjOOl5SLKs",
          "author": "@klicclak",
          "authorImage": "https://yt3.ggpht.com/P0mLUid3qrsfuzaJfFCupB1QRK4dk0u_7SVSs9OFOTR-7JgAGHB7zpQ6g30NRfNd42j3--xN=s48-c-k-c0x00ffffff-no-rj",
          "text": "They don't use the latin alphabet. There is official english spellings for proper nouns like cities and provincial names, but the romanization vs pronunciation history of hangul is not a short answer. \n\nThe shortest possible answer I can give you: the korean letter ㄱ is pronounced somewhere between K and G (on a spectrum) depending on many factors. There is no exact english equivalent for this letter so official romanizations can vary",
          "likeCount": 2,
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-03T03:09:43Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "Ugy6CuP9hb55RH7cBtV4AaABAg.AcjCweTqbBMAcjOBCIAgSV",
